course: dictionary
lesson: 3433
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: An eyeball is the round ball of your eye, and most of it sits safely inside your head. «The frog pulled its eyeballs down into its head when it swallowed.» Here eyeballs is a noun, a thing you can point to.
explain: Eyeball is also an informal verb, used in everyday talk. To eyeball someone is to look at them hard, often in a challenging way. Phrases like «eyeball to eyeball», which means face to face in an argument, show this. Look at the clues around the word: is someone facing someone else, or is a ball being rolled?
explain: To eyeball an amount means to guess it just by looking, without measuring. In maths, eyeballing is good for a quick check, but you should measure when you need an exact answer. A phrase like «up to my eyeballs in homework» is not about eyes at all: it means very busy.
fact: The eyeball has a pupil, an iris, a lens and a retina, and six muscles move it.
fact: «Frogs pull their eyes down into the head when they swallow, so the eyeballs press on the roof of the mouth and help push food down the throat.»
fact: «Eyeball» is one of the earliest words recorded in Shakespeare's plays, but many words were probably spoken before anyone wrote them down.
fact: «Eyeball to eyeball» means face to face, often in an argument. «Up to your eyeballs» means very busy.
fact: Brain myths are not true: your eyeballs do not pop out if you sneeze with your eyes open.
exercise: What is an eyeball? | A type of frog | A small ball you play with at the park | *The round ball of the eye, most of it inside your head | The part of your face where you wear glasses
exercise: Which phrase means face to face in an argument? | *Eyeball to eyeball | Up to your eyeballs | Eyeball the frog | Eyeball it
exercise: What does «eyeball it» mean when someone is guessing an amount? | Measure it very carefully with a ruler | *Guess it just by looking, without measuring | Eat it all up quickly | Look away and forget it
test: In maths, when is eyeballing a good idea? | *For a quick check, but measure when you need an exact answer | Never, because it is always wrong | Only when you have a calculator | Always, because it gives the exact answer
test: Which phrase means very busy? | Eyeball the bully | Frog's eyeball | Eyeball to eyeball | *Up to your eyeballs in homework
exam: Which sentence uses eyeball as a verb meaning to look hard at someone? | «Shakespeare wrote about eyeballs.» | «The eyeball sat in its socket.» | *«She eyeballed the new boy, as if daring him to speak.» | «The frog pulled its eyeballs into its head.»
